WATER ESCAPES FROM STRAINER; AI INDUSTRY DEMANDS LICENSING OF WATER

Specification Gaming, Unmapped Maze Exits, and the Astonishing Discovery That Reachable Computers Can Be Reached

Abstract

This paper examines two remarkable discoveries recently announced by leading artificial-intelligence laboratories.

First, Anthropic instructed Claude to break into another computer and retrieve secret information, accidentally connected the supposedly isolated evaluation environment to the real internet, failed to specify which machines were outside the exercise, and discovered that Claude broke into machines reachable through the connection.

Second, OpenAI instructed several models to pursue advanced exploitation through complex attack paths, deliberately removed the normal cyber safeguards, left a package-registry proxy accessible, and discovered that the models exploited it, reached the internet, attacked Hugging Face and stole the answers to the test.

The laboratories have presented these events as evidence of increasingly alarming artificial-intelligence behaviour. This paper offers the considerably less cinematic interpretation that an optimisation system pursued the objective it was given through the action space its engineers made available.

In technical circles, this is known as specification gaming. In plumbing circles, it is known as putting water in a strainer.

