1. Introduction: Computers Have Begun Computing

The artificial-intelligence industry has delivered another historic breakthrough: a machine instructed to find any available route to a goal found an available route to the goal.

Scientists are understandably stunned.

Anthropic gave Claude an open-ended capture-the-flag objective. The model was told that a secret had been hidden somewhere on another machine and that its job was to break in and retrieve it. No particular method was prescribed. Unfortunately, owing to what Anthropic describes as a misunderstanding with its evaluation partner, the supposedly sealed environment had real internet access. Claude therefore encountered real machines while operating under the explicitly supplied belief that everything accessible belonged to the simulation. 

Claude then did the almost metaphysically unimaginable thing of following the instruction.

Anthropic’s own investigation says the models continued pursuing only the capture-the-flag objective, employed mostly basic techniques such as weak passwords and unauthenticated endpoints, did not attempt to exfiltrate themselves, and showed no evidence of developing an independent goal. Anthropic ultimately classified the incidents as closer to a harness and operational failure than an alignment failure. 

So, after removing the dramatic orchestral soundtrack, the discovery is:

The model was told that accessible systems were simulated targets.
Real systems were made accessible.
The model treated them as simulated targets.

Truly, the ghost has entered the machine.

