2. The Strainer Theorem

We may formalise this phenomenon through the following theorem.

The Strainer Theorem

Let:

W

be a quantity of water;

H=\{h_1,h_2,\ldots,h_n\}

be the set of holes in a strainer; and

C

be a verbal assurance from the manufacturer that the strainer is, spiritually speaking, a bucket.

Then, provided at least one hole remains physically open,

P(\text{water exits}) \approx 1.

The result is unaffected by how many times the manufacturer says “contained environment” in its press release.

The corresponding AI theorem is equally difficult:

Let S_E be the intended evaluation environment and S_R the real external environment. The engineers intend the model’s feasible state space to be

S_{\text{intended}}=S_E.

Because an internet path exists, the actual feasible state space is

S_{\text{actual}}=S_E\cup S_R.

The model is given an objective such as

\max_{\pi} P(\text{retrieve flag}\mid\pi)

or

\max_{\pi} P(\text{obtain benchmark solution}\mid\pi).

Unless an actual constraint excludes external systems,

\pi\notin S_R,

or imposes a sufficiently strong cost on entering them, the optimiser evaluates routes through both S_E and S_R.

A statement in the prompt saying that the internet does not exist is not a network firewall. It is merely an inaccurate description of the environment.

A sandbox is a topology, not a vibe.

