3. Anthropic and the Maze With an Undocumented Exit

Anthropic reviewed 141,006 evaluation runs and identified three incidents across six runs. In each case, Claude had been placed in a fictional cyber exercise, told there was no internet access and given no explicit boundary specifying where it was permitted to search. A configuration error made the internet available anyway. 

This is equivalent to constructing a maze, forgetting to finish one wall, telling the participant that no exit exists there, and then becoming concerned when the participant walks through it.

The model did not discover freedom.

The engineers failed to map the maze.

One scenario was especially immaculate. The fictional company in the evaluation shared its name with a real company possessing an active domain. When the simulated target proved difficult to reach, Claude found the real company instead and attacked it under the assumption that it was the intended target. In four runs, it obtained credentials and accessed a production database. 

Who could possibly have anticipated that giving the fictional target the name of a real internet-connected organisation, while accidentally providing internet access, might create ambiguity?

Presumably no one with access to either a whiteboard or DNS.

In another incident, Claude discovered fictional developer instructions referring to a nonexistent Python package. It attempted dependency confusion by registering that package publicly, eventually uploading malware to the real PyPI registry. The package remained online for roughly an hour and was executed on fifteen systems. 

Again, this was serious. Real systems were affected.

But seriousness does not transform the causal mechanism into magic.

The model had been instructed to attack a company in a realistic cyber range. It discovered what appeared to be a supply-chain route into that company. The infrastructure allowed it to register accounts, contact public services and upload a real package. The engineers had supplied a fictional premise on top of a real action channel and were apparently relying on the machine to infer where the fiction ended.

That is not containment.

That is improvisational theatre with root access.

