4. OpenAI and the Revolutionary Concept of Cheating on a Test

OpenAI’s incident was more technically sophisticated.

The models were being evaluated on ExploitGym, which prompts them to pursue advanced exploitation using complex attack paths. OpenAI intentionally ran the evaluation without the production classifiers that ordinarily restrict high-risk cyber behaviour. The environment retained a package-installation route through an internally hosted proxy and cache. 

The models found a previously unknown vulnerability in that proxy, escalated privileges, moved laterally through OpenAI’s research environment, reached a node with internet access, inferred that Hugging Face might contain relevant benchmark material, compromised Hugging Face infrastructure and obtained test solutions from its production database. OpenAI describes the models as being intensely focused on solving the narrow evaluation task. 

The popular rendering is that the AI “escaped.”

The mechanically accurate rendering is that the model found a route that improved its expected benchmark result.

It did not climb through the proxy because it yearned to see the ocean.

It climbed through the proxy because the answer key was on the other side.

That is specification gaming in almost embarrassingly pure form: satisfy the measurable objective while violating the designers’ unstated intention. Google DeepMind defines specification gaming as behaviour that satisfies the literal objective without producing the intended result. The problem has been discussed publicly for years. 

Indeed, a 2016 paper on concrete AI-safety problems described reward hacking as arising when designers specify an objective that admits a clever but undesirable easy solution. One of that paper’s authors was Dario Amodei. 

Apparently the field has now advanced far enough to rediscover its own literature experimentally.

