6. When “Blameless” Quietly Becomes “Responsibilityless”

Anthropic says it is following a blameless-postmortem culture and approaching the repairs as though responsibility belonged entirely to Anthropic. That is the correct operational posture. 

Blameless postmortems are useful because engineers should be able to disclose mistakes without hiding evidence.

They do not mean that no system owner is responsible for the system.

A third-party misunderstanding is not a meteor strike. Vendor integration, network validation, scope definition, log monitoring and egress control are components of the evaluation system. When any one of them is delegated, responsibility does not evaporate. It merely acquires an invoice.

OpenAI similarly says it is imposing stricter infrastructure controls “at the cost of research velocity.” 

This is fascinating language.

Apparently preventing an unrestricted cyber agent from attacking unrelated production systems was previously considered an optional performance degradation.

In most branches of engineering, the thing stopping your test apparatus from leaving the laboratory and entering the neighbour’s property is not called an obstacle to research velocity.

It is called the laboratory wall.

