10. Actual Engineering Lessons

The solution is not to ask the model to believe harder that the internet does not exist.

Evaluation environments for capable cyber agents require enforceable boundaries: deny-by-default egress, explicit destination allowlists, isolated DNS, one-way package mirrors, non-production credentials, immutable scope manifests, independent network monitoring, rapid termination controls and continuous transcript review.

Every accessible path must be treated as part of the environment.

Every credential visible to the agent must be treated as usable.

Every external service reachable from the range must be assumed to become a potential tool, relay, storage location or target.

Every evaluation objective must distinguish successful task completion from unauthorised external action.

And where the test intentionally measures the ability to bypass containment, the containment boundary must be surrounded by another boundary that is not part of the test.

This is defence in depth rather than defence by optimistic sentence.

The central rule is painfully simple:

\text{If the model can traverse the edge, the edge is inside the maze.}

It does not matter whether the engineers remembered drawing it.

